    "label": "Operator Precedence, Associativity & Short-Circuit Evaluation",
    "name": "Operator Precedence",
    "category": "C Programming Fundamentals",
    "kind": "definition",
    "summary": "Rules governing the evaluation order of expressions in C. Precedence resolves which operator binds first; associativity resolves operators of equal precedence. Logical operators (`&&`, `||`) guarantee sequence points and short-circuit evaluation from left to right.",
    "formula": "\\text{Postfix } (\\texttt{++}, \\texttt{--}) > \\text{Prefix } (\\texttt{++}, \\texttt{*}, \\texttt{\\&}) > \\text{Arithmetic } > \\text{Relational } > \\text{Logical } > \\text{Ternary } > \\text{Assignment}",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Short-circuit } A \\;\\&\\&\\; B: \\text{If } A == 0 \\implies B \\text{ is NEVER evaluated}",
      "\\text{Short-circuit } A \\;||\\; B: \\text{If } A \\ne 0 \\implies B \\text{ is NEVER evaluated}",
      "\\text{Right-to-Left Associativity: } \\text{Unary operators, Conditional } (?:), \\text{ Assignment } (=, +=)"
    ],
    "complexity": "Evaluation: Left-to-right evaluation order except for unary, ternary, and assignments",
    "codeSnippet": "#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 0, b = 5;\n    int res = a++ && ++b; // Short-circuit: ++b is NOT executed because a was 0\n    printf(\"a=%d, b=%d, res=%d\\n\", a, b, res); // a=1, b=5, res=0\n    \n    int x = 1, y = 2, z = 3;\n    int ans = (x += y += z); // Right-to-Left: y += 3 (y=5), x += 5 (x=6)\n    printf(\"x=%d, y=%d\\n\", x, y); // x=6, y=5\n    return 0;\n}",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"130\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"420\" height=\"85\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Precedence Hierarchy (High \u2192 Low)</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"68\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"monospace\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Postfix () [] . -> &gt; Unary * &amp; ! ++ -- &gt; * / % &gt; + -</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&gt; Relational &gt; Equality &gt; &amp;&amp; &gt; || &gt; Ternary ?: &gt; Assignment =</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "Undefined Behavior: Modifying a variable multiple times without an intervening sequence point (`i = i++ + ++i;`) has UNDEFINED behavior in C.",
      "Bitwise `&` and `|` have LOWER precedence than relational `==` and `!=` (`x & 1 == 0` evaluates as `x & (1 == 0)`).",
      "Comma operator `,` has the LOWEST precedence of all operators and evaluates left-to-right, returning the rightmost value."
    ],
