    "subjectId": "Computer Networks",
    "chapterId": "cn-routing",
    "label": "Distance Vector Routing",
    "kind": "algorithm",
    "summary": "Each router advertises its full distance vector (destination, cost) to neighbors. RIP uses hop count metric (max 15, 16=infinity). Updates are periodic (30s). Suffers from count-to-infinity problem - solved by split horizon and poison reverse. Convergence is slow.",
    "formula": "D(x,y) = \\min_v \\{ c(x,v) + D(v,y) \\}",
    "pseudocode": "# Simple distance-vector update\ndef update(table, neighbor_vector, link_cost):\n    for dest, cost in neighbor_vector.items():\n        new_cost = link_cost + cost\n        if dest not in table or new_cost < table[dest]:\n            table[dest] = new_cost\n    return table",
    "prerequisites": [],
    "examRelevance": 5,
    "commonTraps": [
      "RIP metric is hop count only - not bandwidth or delay.",
      "Count-to-infinity occurs because routers advertise cost back to the neighbor they learned from; split horizon prevents this."
    ],
