    "id": "c-em-gradient-directional-derivative",
    "subjectId": "Engineering Mathematics",
    "chapterId": "em-calculus",
    "label": "Gradient Vector $\\nabla f$, Directional Derivatives & Tangent Planes",
    "name": "Gradient & Directional Derivatives",
    "category": "Calculus",
    "kind": "technique",
    "summary": "Vector differential calculus: 1. Gradient Vector: $\\nabla f = \\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} \\right)$ points in the direction of MAXIMUM RATE OF INCREASE of $f$; 2. Maximum Rate of Increase equals the magnitude of the gradient: $\\|\\nabla f\\|$; 3. Directional Derivative along unit vector $\\hat{u}$: $D_{\\hat{u}} f = \\nabla f \\cdot \\hat{u} = \\|\\nabla f\\| \\cos \\theta$; 4. Normal Vector to Level Surface $f(x, y, z) = c$ is $\\vec{n} = \\nabla f$; 5. Tangent Plane Equation at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$: $f_x (x-x_0) + f_y (y-y_0) + f_z (z-z_0) = 0$.",
    "formula": "D_{\\hat{u}} f = \\nabla f \\cdot \\hat{u} \\quad | \\quad \\text{Max Increase} = \\|\\nabla f\\| \\; (\\text{in direction of } \\nabla f) \\quad | \\quad \\vec{n} = \\nabla f",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Gradient: } \\nabla f = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\hat{i} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\hat{j} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} \\hat{k}",
      "\\text{Directional Derivative: } D_u f = \\nabla f \\cdot \\frac{\\vec{u}}{\\|\\vec{u}\\|} \\; (\\text{Must normalize } u \\text{ to UNIT vector!})",
      "\\text{Maximum Rate of Increase: } +\\|\\nabla f\\| \\; (\\theta = 0^\\circ); \\quad \\text{Maximum Rate of Decrease: } -\\|\\nabla f\\| \\; (\\theta = 180^\\circ)",
      "\\text{Zero Rate of Change: } D_u f = 0 \\; (\\theta = 90^\\circ, \\text{ tangent to level curve})",
      "\\text{Tangent Plane Equation: } \\nabla f(x_0, y_0, z_0) \\cdot (r - r_0) = 0"
    ],
    "complexity": "Gradient Evaluation: $O(d)$ scalar products for $d$ dimensions",
    "codeSnippet": "# Directional Derivative in Python\nimport numpy as np\n# f(x, y) = x^2 * y at point (1, 2) in direction of vector v = (3, 4)\n# grad f = (2xy, x^2) -> at (1, 2): grad f = (4, 1)\ngrad_f = np.array([4.0, 1.0])\nv = np.array([3.0, 4.0])\nu_hat = v / np.linalg.norm(v) # (0.6, 0.8)\ndir_deriv = np.dot(grad_f, u_hat) # 4*(0.6) + 1*(0.8) = 3.2\nprint('Directional Derivative:', dir_deriv)",
