    "id": "c-em-bayes-theorem",
    "subjectId": "Engineering Mathematics",
    "chapterId": "em-probability",
    "label": "Bayes' Theorem, Prior vs Posterior & False Positive Paradox",
    "name": "Bayes' Theorem",
    "category": "Probability & Statistics",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "summary": "Calculates posterior probability of cause $B_k$ given observed evidence / effect $A$: $P(B_k \\mid A) = \\frac{P(A \\mid B_k) P(B_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(A \\mid B_i) P(B_i)} = \\frac{P(A \\mid B_k) P(B_k)}{P(A)}$. Prior Probability: $P(B_k)$ (Belief before evidence); Likelihood: $P(A \\mid B_k)$; Posterior Probability: $P(B_k \\mid A)$ (Updated belief after evidence). False Positive Paradox: When base rate $P(B)$ is very small (e.g. rare disease $0.1\\%$), even a test with $99\\%$ accuracy produces mostly false positives.",
    "formula": "P(B_k \\mid A) = \\frac{P(A \\mid B_k) P(B_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(A \\mid B_i) P(B_i)} \\quad | \\quad \\text{Posterior} = \\frac{\\text{Likelihood} \\times \\text{Prior}}{\\text{Evidence}}",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Bayes' Formula (2 Hypotheses): } P(B \\mid A) = \\frac{P(A \\mid B) P(B)}{P(A \\mid B) P(B) + P(A \\mid B') P(B')}",
      "\\text{Medical Testing: Sensitivity } = P(+ \\mid \\text{Disease}), \\quad \\text{Specificity } = P(- \\mid \\text{Healthy})",
      "P(\\text{Disease} \\mid +) = \\frac{\\text{Sensitivity} \\times P(\\text{Disease})}{\\text{Sensitivity} \\times P(\\text{Disease}) + (1 - \\text{Specificity}) \\times P(\\text{Healthy})}"
    ],
    "complexity": "Inference: $O(k)$ computation over $k$ discrete causes",
    "codeSnippet": "# Bayes' Theorem in Python (Rare Disease Test)\ndef bayes_disease_test(prior_disease=0.001, sensitivity=0.99, specificity=0.99):\n    prior_healthy = 1.0 - prior_disease\n    p_pos_given_healthy = 1.0 - specificity # False positive rate = 0.01\n    p_pos = (sensitivity * prior_disease) + (p_pos_given_healthy * prior_healthy)\n    posterior = (sensitivity * prior_disease) / p_pos\n    return posterior # ~0.0901 (Only 9% chance person has disease despite positive test!)\nprint('Posterior Probability:', bayes_disease_test())",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bayes' Theorem</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">P(B|A) = P(A|B)P(B) / P(A)</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#22C55E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Prior \u2192 Posterior</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Base rate dominates rare events</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "In medical diagnostic questions, ALWAYS account for the low base rate (prior probability $P(\\text{Disease})$) \u2014 neglecting base rate is the most common error in GATE probability questions.",
      "The denominator of Bayes' formula is simply the Law of Total Probability $P(A)$."
    ],
