    "id": "c-ga-ratio-proportion",
    "subjectId": "General Aptitude",
    "chapterId": "ga-quantitative",
    "label": "Ratio, Proportion, Variations & Partnership Profit Sharing",
    "name": "Ratio & Proportion",
    "category": "Quantitative Aptitude",
    "kind": "technique",
    "summary": "Techniques for proportional reasoning: 1. Ratio Scaling: If $A:B = a:b$ and $B:C = c:d$, then $A:B:C = (a \\cdot c) : (b \\cdot c) : (b \\cdot d)$; 2. Proportions: $a:b :: c:d \\implies a d = b c$ (Product of Extremes $=$ Product of Means); 3. Variations: Direct Variation ($y = k x$), Inverse Variation ($y = k/x$), Joint Variation ($y = k x z$); 4. Partnership Profit Sharing: Profit ratio equals ratio of products of Investment and Time: $\\text{Profit}_A : \\text{Profit}_B = (I_A \\times T_A) : (I_B \\times T_B)$.",
    "formula": "\\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{c}{d} \\implies ad = bc \\quad | \\quad \\text{Profit}_A : \\text{Profit}_B = (I_A \\times T_A) : (I_B \\times T_B)",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Combining Ratios } (A:B=2:3, B:C=4:5): A:B:C = (2\\times 4) : (3\\times 4) : (3\\times 5) = 8 : 12 : 15",
      "\\text{Mean Proportional of } a \\text{ and } b: x = \\sqrt{ab}",
      "\\text{Third Proportional to } a, b: c = \\frac{b^2}{a}",
      "\\text{Componendo and Dividendo: } \\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{c}{d} \\implies \\frac{a+b}{a-b} = \\frac{c+d}{c-d}",
      "\\text{Partnership Profit: } P_1 : P_2 = (I_1 \\cdot T_1) : (I_2 \\cdot T_2)"
    ],
    "complexity": "Evaluation: Cross-multiplication in $O(1)$",
    "codeSnippet": "-- Combining Ratios Example (A:B = 3:4, B:C = 8:9, C:D = 15:16):\n-- A:B:C = (3*2) : (4*2) : 9 = 6 : 8 : 9\n-- A:B:C:D = (6*5) : (8*5) : (9*5) : (3*16) = 30 : 40 : 45 : 48\n-- Simplify: 10 : 13.33... -> Direct A:D = (3/4)*(8/9)*(15/16) = 5/8 -> A:D = 5:8!",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Product of Extremes</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">a \u00b7 d = b \u00b7 c</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#22C55E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Partnership Profit</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">P1 : P2 = (I1 \u00b7 T1) : (I2 \u00b7 T2)</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "In partnership problems, NEVER divide profit solely by investment amount \u2014 you MUST multiply investment by the number of active months ($I \\times T$).",
      "Do not forget to scale intermediate terms when merging ratios $A:B$ and $B:C$."
    ],
