    "id": "c-dbms-lossless-join",
    "subjectId": "DBMS",
    "chapterId": "dbms-normalization",
    "label": "Lossless / Non-Lossy Join Decomposition & Testing",
    "name": "Lossless Join Decomposition",
    "category": "Database Design & Normalization",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "summary": "A decomposition of relation $R$ into $R_1, R_2$ is Lossless (Non-Lossy) if natural join of decompositions reproduces exactly the original relation ($R_1 \\bowtie R_2 = R$). For binary decomposition, lossless IF AND ONLY IF common attributes ($R_1 \\cap R_2$) form a Super Key in AT LEAST ONE sub-relation.",
    "formula": "R_1 \\bowtie R_2 = R \\iff (R_1 \\cap R_2 \\to R_1) \\in F^+ \\lor (R_1 \\cap R_2 \\to R_2) \\in F^+",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Binary Decomposition Lossless Condition: } (R_1 \\cap R_2)^+ \\supseteq R_1 \\lor (R_1 \\cap R_2)^+ \\supseteq R_2",
      "\\text{Lossy Decomposition: } R_1 \\bowtie R_2 \\supset R \\; (\\text{Generates spurious / fake tuples})",
      "\\text{In general: } R \\subseteq R_1 \\bowtie R_2 \\bowtie \\dots \\bowtie R_k \\; (\\text{Decomposition NEVER loses tuples, only ADDS fake ones})",
      "\\text{Multi-Relation Decomposition: Tested via the Chase Tableau Algorithm with 'a' and 'b' symbols}"
    ],
    "complexity": "Binary Lossless Test: $O(|F| \\cdot |\\text{attrs}|)$ via attribute closure of $(R_1 \\cap R_2)$",
    "codeSnippet": "# Binary Lossless Join Check in Python\ndef is_lossless_binary(r1, r2, fds):\n    common = set(r1).intersection(set(r2))\n    if not common: return False # Empty intersection is always lossy!\n    closure = compute_closure(common, fds)\n    return set(r1).issubset(closure) or set(r2).issubset(closure)",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#22C55E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lossless Join</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(R1 \u2229 R2) \u2192 R1 OR R2</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#EF4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lossy Join</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Produces Spurious / Fake Tuples</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "A decomposition can NEVER LOSE original tuples ($R \\subseteq R_1 \\bowtie R_2$ is always true); a 'lossy' decomposition actually CREATES SPURIOUS FAKE TUPLES ($R_1 \\bowtie R_2 \\supset R$).",
      "If $R_1 \\cap R_2 = \\emptyset$ (no common attributes), the decomposition is GUARANTEED TO BE LOSSY (Cartesian product produces maximum spurious tuples)."
    ],
