    "label": "Regular Grammars (Right-Linear vs Left-Linear) & Equivalence",
    "name": "Regular Grammars",
    "category": "Finite Automata & Regular Languages",
    "kind": "definition",
    "summary": "Type 3 Grammars generating regular languages. Right-Linear Grammar (RLG): All productions in form $A \\to wB$ or $A \\to w$ ($w \\in T^*$). Left-Linear Grammar (LLG): All productions in form $A \\to Bw$ or $A \\to w$. A grammar mixing both right-linear and left-linear productions can generate NON-REGULAR languages (e.g. $S \\to aB, B \\to Sb$ generates $a^n b^n$).",
    "formula": "\\text{RLG: } A \\to wB \\mid w \\quad | \\quad \\text{LLG: } A \\to Bw \\mid w \\quad (w \\in T^*, A, B \\in V)",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Pure Right-Linear Grammar } \\implies \\text{Generates REGULAR language}",
      "\\text{Pure Left-Linear Grammar } \\implies \\text{Generates REGULAR language}",
      "\\text{Mixed Linear Grammar (Linear Grammar): May generate Context-Free / Non-Regular languages}",
      "\\text{RLG to NFA: Production } A \\to aB \\text{ maps directly to transition } \\delta(A, a) = B"
    ],
    "complexity": "Direct mapping between RLG productions and NFA transitions in $O(|P|)$",
    "codeSnippet": "-- Pure Right-Linear Grammar for (a+b)*a:\n-- S -> aS | bS | a\n\n-- Linear Grammar (NOT regular): generates {a^n b^n | n >= 1}\n-- S -> aSb | ab (Mixes variables on both sides -> CFG!)",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Right-Linear: A \u2192 wB | w</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Guaranteed Regular</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#EF4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mixed Linear (A \u2192 aBb)</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Can generate Non-Regular (a^n b^n)</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "A grammar having SOME right-linear productions and SOME left-linear productions is called a LINEAR grammar and is NOT necessarily regular (e.g. $S \\to aA, A \\to Sb \\mid ab$ is non-regular).",
      "Regular grammars must be STRICTLY all Right-Linear OR strictly all Left-Linear."
    ],
