    "id": "c-toc-halting-problem",
    "subjectId": "TOC",
    "chapterId": "toc-computability",
    "label": "The Halting Problem of Turing Machines ($HALT_{TM}$)",
    "name": "Halting Problem Undecidability",
    "category": "Turing Machines & Computability Theory",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "summary": "Alan Turing's seminal 1936 proof of uncomputability. The problem: Given an arbitrary Turing Machine $M$ and input $w$, decide whether $M$ will eventually halt on $w$. Language $HALT_{TM} = \\{ \\langle M, w \\rangle \\mid M \\text{ halts on } w \\}$. $HALT_{TM}$ is Recursively Enumerable (RE), but PROVEN UNDECIDABLE via self-referential diagonal contradiction.",
    "formula": "HALT_{TM} = \\{ \\langle M, w \\rangle \\mid M \\text{ halts on input } w \\} \\in \\text{RE} - \\text{REC}",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Proof by Contradiction: Assume Decider } H(\\langle M, w \\rangle) \\text{ exists that returns YES if } M(w) \\text{ halts, NO if loops}",
      "\\text{Construct Adversarial TM } D(\\langle M \\rangle): \\text{If } H(\\langle M, \\langle M \\rangle \\rangle) = \\text{YES } \\to \\text{Loop Forever; If NO } \\to \\text{Halt}",
      "\\text{Evaluate } D(\\langle D \\rangle): D \\text{ halts } \\iff D \\text{ loops forever! Contradiction!}",
      "\\text{Conclusion: No such decider } H \\text{ can possibly exist. Hence } HALT_{TM} \\text{ is UNDECIDABLE}."
    ],
    "complexity": "Undecidability: No algorithmic solution exists in any programming language",
    "codeSnippet": "# Turing's Halting Contradiction in Python:\ndef D(M_code):\n    if H(M_code, M_code) == 'HALTS':\n        while True: pass # Loop forever\n    else:\n        return 'HALT' # Halt immediately\n# What does D(D_code) do? Contradiction proves H cannot exist!",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">HALT_TM \u2208 RE</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Semi-Decidable (Run TM)</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#EF4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">HALT_TM \u2209 REC</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">UNDECIDABLE (Diagonalization)</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "$HALT_{TM}$ is UNDECIDABLE, but it IS Recursively Enumerable (RE) because a Universal TM can simulate $M(w)$ and accept if it halts.",
      "The complement of Halting Problem $\\overline{HALT}_{TM}$ is STRICTLY NON-RE."
    ],
