    "id": "c-toc-reductions",
    "subjectId": "TOC",
    "chapterId": "toc-computability",
    "label": "Many-One Reductions ($A \\le_m B$) & Undecidability Proofs",
    "name": "Many-One Reductions",
    "category": "Turing Machines & Computability Theory",
    "kind": "technique",
    "summary": "Primary technique to prove problems undecidable. A language $A$ is Many-One Reducible to $B$ ($A \\le_m B$) if there exists a computable function $f: \\Sigma^* \\to \\Sigma^*$ such that $w \\in A \\iff f(w) \\in B$. If $A \\le_m B$: 1. $B$ is Decidable $\\implies A$ is Decidable; 2. $A$ is Undecidable $\\implies B$ is Undecidable; 3. $A$ is Non-RE $\\implies B$ is Non-RE.",
    "formula": "A \\le_m B \\iff \\exists \\text{ computable } f \\text{ s.t. } w \\in A \\iff f(w) \\in B",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Direction Rule 1 (Decidability): } B \\in \\text{REC} \\implies A \\in \\text{REC}",
      "\\text{Direction Rule 2 (Undecidability): } A \\notin \\text{REC} \\implies B \\notin \\text{REC} \\; (\\text{Reduce FROM known hard problem } A)",
      "\\text{Direction Rule 3 (Recognizability): } B \\in \\text{RE} \\implies A \\in \\text{RE}",
      "\\text{Direction Rule 4 (Non-RE): } A \\notin \\text{RE} \\implies B \\notin \\text{RE}"
    ],
    "complexity": "Reduction Pipeline: To prove $P$ undecidable, reduce $HALT_{TM} \\le_m P$",
    "codeSnippet": "# Many-One Reduction Template:\n# To prove Problem B is undecidable:\n# 1. Choose known undecidable Problem A (e.g. HALT_TM)\n# 2. Construct computable function f converting instance <M, w> of A to instance <M'> of B\n# 3. Prove: <M, w> in A <==> <M'> in B\n# 4. Conclude: B must be undecidable!",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"35\" width=\"100\" height=\"45\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#EF4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#EF4444\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Problem A</text><text x=\"75\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Known Hard</text><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"57\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#22C55E\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"200,57 190,52 190,62\" fill=\"#22C55E\"/><text x=\"162\" y=\"48\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">f(w)</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"35\" width=\"100\" height=\"45\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Problem B</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Target Problem</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"11\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A \u2264_m B</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "DIRECTION ERROR TRAP: To prove $B$ is undecidable, you MUST reduce FROM a known undecidable problem $A$ TO $B$ ($A \\le_m B$). Reducing $B \\le_m A$ proves NOTHING about $B$!",
      "If $A \\le_m B$ and $B$ is undecidable, $A$ may or may not be undecidable ($B$ could be harder than $A$)."
    ],
