    "name": "RISC vs CISC",
    "category": "Instruction Formats & Addressing Modes",
    "kind": "definition",
    "summary": "Architectural paradigms. RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer) focuses on simple, single-cycle, fixed-length instructions, load-store architecture, large register files, and hardwired control. CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer) focuses on rich, variable-length instructions, memory operands in ALU ops, and microprogrammed control.",
    "formula": "\\text{RISC: Simple Instructions } \\times \\text{Higher Instruction Count} \\quad | \\quad \\text{CISC: Lower Instruction Count } \\times \\text{High CPI}",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{RISC: Fixed 32-bit instruction length, Load-Store only (ALU operates on registers only), Hardwired control, Heavy pipelining}",
      "\\text{CISC: Variable instruction length (1 to 15 bytes), Memory-to-Memory ALU ops, Microprogrammed control, Few registers}"
    ],
    "complexity": "CPU Performance equation tradeoff: $\\text{Time} = I \\times \\text{CPI} \\times \\tau$",
    "codeSnippet": "// RISC Load-Store Execution:\n// LOAD R1, A\n// LOAD R2, B\n// ADD R3, R1, R2\n// STORE C, R3\n// CISC Single-Instruction Equivalent: ADD C, A, B (Direct memory manipulation)",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">RISC (ARM, MIPS, RISC-V)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fixed Length, Load-Store, Hardwired</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#F59E0B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#F59E0B\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CISC (x86, VAX)</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Variable Length, Memory ALU, Microcode</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "In pure RISC, ALU instructions NEVER take memory addresses as operands; all memory access is restricted strictly to `LOAD` and `STORE`.",
      "CISC programs have smaller binary code size (fewer instructions), but RISC executes faster due to pipelining efficiency ($CPI \\approx 1$)."
    ],
