    "kind": "technique",
    "summary": "Cross-Compiler: A compiler running on host machine $H$ that produces target machine code for a different architecture $T$. Represented via T-Diagrams $S \\xrightarrow{I} T$: translates source language $S$ to target language $T$, written in implementation language $I$. Bootstrapping: Process of writing a compiler in its own source language ($S=I$) to compile itself.",
    "formula": "\\text{T-Diagram: } S \\; \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|} \\hline S & & T \\\\ \\hline & I & \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\; T",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{T-Diagram Composition: Compiler } A \\xrightarrow{I_1} B \\text{ running on machine } I_1 \\text{ can compile compiler } B \\xrightarrow{A} C",
      "\\text{Bootstrapping Rule: To bootstrap a compiler for language } L \\text{ on machine } M:",
      "1. \\text{Write small core compiler for subset } L_0 \\text{ in machine code } M",
      "2. \\text{Write compiler for full language } L \\text{ in } L_0 \\text{ and compile using step 1}"
    ],
    "complexity": "Porting: Allows bringing a new programming language to any new hardware architecture",
    "codeSnippet": "-- Bootstrapping Concept:\n-- Step 1: Write C compiler in Assembly (C_to_x86 written in Assembly)\n-- Step 2: Write C compiler in C (C_to_x86 written in C)\n-- Step 3: Run Step 1 compiler on Step 2 source code -> Output: Native C_to_x86 executable!",
