    "id": "c-dl-state-machines",
    "subjectId": "Digital Logic",
    "chapterId": "dl-counters-fsm",
    "label": "Finite State Machines (FSM): Mealy vs Moore Machine Architectures",
    "name": "Mealy & Moore FSMs",
    "category": "Synchronous/Asynchronous Counters & FSM",
    "kind": "definition",
    "summary": "Synchronous sequential circuits modeled as state machines: 1. Mealy Machine: Output is a function of BOTH Present State AND Present Input: $Z(t) = g(S(t), X(t))$. Outputs can change asynchronously with input changes during clock cycle. Requires FEWER states; 2. Moore Machine: Output is a function of PRESENT STATE ONLY: $Z(t) = h(S(t))$. Outputs are strictly synchronous with clock edges (Glitch-free). Converting $N$-state Mealy to Moore requires up to $N \\times |\\Sigma|$ states.",
    "formula": "\\text{Mealy: } Z(t) = g(S(t), X(t)) \\quad | \\quad \\text{Moore: } Z(t) = h(S(t))",
    "keyFormulae": [
      "\\text{Mealy Machine: Output associated with TRANSITION ARROWS } (X/Z)",
      "\\text{Moore Machine: Output associated with STATE CIRCLES } (S / Z)",
      "\\text{State Count: } |Q_{\\text{Moore}}| \\ge |Q_{\\text{Mealy}}| \\; (\\text{Moore machine typically requires } 1 \\text{ extra state})",
      "\\text{Timing: Moore outputs change ONLY on clock edges; Mealy outputs can glitch on input changes}"
    ],
    "complexity": "Hardware Tradeoff: Mealy has faster response (0 delay); Moore has higher clock speed and glitch-free outputs",
    "codeSnippet": "-- Mealy vs Moore State Diagram Syntax:\n-- Mealy Transition:  State_A --(Input=1 / Output=0)--> State_B\n-- Moore Transition:  (State_A / Out=0) --(Input=1)--> (State_B / Out=1)",
    "imageUrl": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"460\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#0F172A\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mealy Machine</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#CBD5E1\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Output = f(State, Input) | Fewer States</text><rect x=\"245\" y=\"20\" width=\"190\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1E293B\" stroke=\"#22C55E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Moore Machine</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#22C55E\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Output = f(State) | Glitch-Free</text></svg>",
    "commonTraps": [
      "For a sequence detector of length $L$: Mealy machine requires $L$ states; Moore machine requires $L+1$ states.",
      "Mealy machine outputs can react immediately to input changes in the middle of a clock period."
    ],
